  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Device Registration & MFA", "technique": "T1098.005", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 08:02:14 CEST, Wazuh's Office365/Azure module logged a Microsoft Graph authentication-method registration event. data.srcip=194.165.16.77 issued a POST against /v1.0/me/authentication/phoneMethods carrying a stolen bearer token (UPN alice.jones@corp.com) and registered a new mobile phone MFA method (+1-555-0184). The critical anomaly: there was NO preceding interactive sign-in for alice.jones from this IP — the security-info change was driven purely by a bearer token, which is the signature of token theft from an infostealer log rather than a legitimate user updating their own MFA. Wazuh fired custom rule 92310 ('MFA method registered by bearer token (no interactive login)') and 92311 ('Graph API: phoneMethods POST from unknown IP'). Within the same minute the same srcip also POSTed /v1.0/me/authentication/microsoftAuthenticatorMethods to add an attacker-controlled authenticator app named 'Attacker Phone'.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'office365' and 'azure', and rule.description matched 'User registered security info' / 'authentication method registered' with rule.mitre.id=T1098.005 and data.srcip=194.165.16.77. The univariate signature: a security-info / MFA-method registration event for a user with no matching interactive sign-in event from the same source in the preceding window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:02:14+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Cross-platform MFA enrollment", "technique": "T1098.005", "what_happened": "At 08:05:48 CEST, the same data.srcip=194.165.16.77 pivoted to the Google Workspace Admin SDK. It first issued GET /admin/directory/v1/users/alice.jones@corp.com?projection=full to read the account's current 2-step-verification posture, then POSTed /admin/directory/v1/users/alice.jones@corp.com/verificationCodes to enroll an attacker-controlled backup phone (+15550184) as a second 2SV factor. Wazuh fired rule 92313 ('Google Workspace: 2SV enrollment from non-corporate IP'). This is the same MFA-persistence move replicated on a second identity platform — the attacker is widening the persistence footprint so a single-platform remediation does not evict them.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'gcp' / 'google_workspace' and rule.description matched '2-step verification enrollment' / '2sv_enroll' with rule.mitre.id=T1098.005 and data.srcip=194.165.16.77 against the same target user alice.jones@corp.com seen in step 1. The cross-platform repetition of the MFA-add pattern within minutes is the persistence-widening signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:05:48+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — TOTP software token seeding", "technique": "T1098.005", "what_happened": "At 08:09:22 CEST, data.srcip=194.165.16.77 returned to Microsoft Graph and POSTed /v1.0/me/authentication/softwareOathMethods to register an attacker-controlled TOTP (RFC 6238) software token seed against alice.jones@corp.com. Wazuh fired rule 92311 again ('Graph API: authentication method POST from unknown IP') alongside a higher-level correlation. A software OATH seed is the most durable MFA-persistence variant: it requires no phone number, no device, and is invisible to the victim unless they audit their registered methods. Three distinct MFA methods (phone, authenticator app, TOTP seed) are now attacker-controlled across two identity platforms.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'office365'/'azure' and rule.description matched 'authentication method registered' / 'softwareOath' with rule.mitre.id=T1098.005 and data.srcip=194.165.16.77 targeting alice.jones@corp.com. The third MFA-method registration from the same source against the same user inside a 7-minute window confirms a deliberate, methodical persistence-establishment campaign.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:09:22+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Initial Access — Valid Accounts sign-in from new geo", "technique": "T1078.004", "what_happened": "At 08:14:39 CEST, data.srcip=194.165.16.77 issued a POST /common/oauth2/v2.0/token password-grant sign-in for alice.jones@corp.com against the IdP host 10.108.57.184. Because the attacker had just registered their own MFA methods in steps 1-3, this sign-in could satisfy the MFA challenge using an attacker-controlled factor. Wazuh fired rule 92312 ('MFA add followed by login from new geo within 30min'). The Azure AD sign-in was geolocated to the CIS region — anomalous for alice.jones whose baseline sign-in geography is the corporate country. The sequence MFA-registration-then-sign-in from the same non-corporate IP within ~12 minutes is the definitive T1098.005 abuse pattern: the attacker did not just persist, they immediately exercised the persistence.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92312 fired correlating the step 1-3 MFA registrations with a subsequent /oauth2/v2.0/token sign-in from the same data.srcip=194.165.16.77 within a 30-minute window, with rule.description matching 'sign-in from new geo' / 'anomalous location' and rule.mitre.id including T1078.004. The same-IP, same-user, MFA-add-then-login chain closes the loop from persistence to active account takeover.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:14:39+0200"}
  ],
